---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "What happens if a CA is hacked?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Browsers revoke trust in the CA or its intermediates; certificates stop working. CAs have died this way, which is why the system self-corrects." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Why do certificates go into public logs?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Certificate Transparency makes every issuance observable, so fraudulent certificates for your domain become detectable within hours." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Can a CA issue for any domain?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Only after domain-control validation, and only if your CAA records permit that CA." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "What is a root program?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "The policy bundle a browser or OS maintains: which roots are trusted, and under what requirements (Chrome, Mozilla, Microsoft, Apple each run one)." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Do I need a paid certificate for e-commerce?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "No: DV certificates provide identical encryption. Compliance and customer perception, not crypto, are the arguments for paid tiers." } }
  ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Certificate Authorities and the Web of Trust"
  description="Certificate authorities explained: how CAs vouch for certificates, the web of trust, validation levels, and CAA records that constrain issuance."
  schema={[faqSchema]}
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["SSL/TLS Guides", "/ssl-tls/"], ["Certificate Authorities"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/ssl-tls/" class="hover:text-accent">SSL/TLS Guides</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">Certificate Authorities</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">Certificate Authorities Explained</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">A Certificate Authority (CA) is an organization that browsers pre-trust to vouch for the link between a domain and a public key. Every HTTPS connection starts with that trust: the CA is the third party that makes first contact with a stranger safe.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">CA validation levels</h2>
    <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
          <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Verifies</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">DV</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Domain control</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">OV</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Plus organization</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">EV</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Full legal entity</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#how-the-trust-works">How the trust works</a></li>
        <li><a href="#validation-levels">Validation levels</a></li>
        <li><a href="#trust-is-enforced-not-assumed">Trust is enforced, not assumed</a></li>
        <li><a href="#frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="how-the-trust-works">How the trust works</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li>Root CAs are embedded in operating systems and browsers (a few dozen organizations).</li><li>Roots sign intermediate CAs; intermediates sign your certificates: a chain from browser trust to your domain.</li><li>CAs verify control of the domain before issuing (email, DNS, or file challenge), then the certificate is public in Certificate Transparency logs.</li></ol>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="validation-levels">Validation levels</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li><strong class="text-ink">Domain Validation (DV):</strong> proof you control the domain. Minutes, free (Let’s Encrypt). All a standard site needs.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">Organization Validation (OV):</strong> adds verified company details.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">Extended Validation (EV):</strong> deepest vetting; browsers no longer visually distinguish it much, so its practical value has narrowed.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="trust-is-enforced-not-assumed">Trust is enforced, not assumed</h2>
      <p>CAs get audited and misissuance gets punished: when a CA fails, browsers distrust them (multiple have died this way). Your levers: <a href="/caa-record/">CAA records</a> restrict which CAs may issue for you, and Certificate Transparency monitoring alerts you when certificates appear that you did not request.</p>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What happens if a CA is hacked?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Browsers revoke trust in the CA or its intermediates; certificates stop working. CAs have died this way, which is why the system self-corrects.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why do certificates go into public logs?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Certificate Transparency makes every issuance observable, so fraudulent certificates for your domain become detectable within hours.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can a CA issue for any domain?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Only after domain-control validation, and only if your CAA records permit that CA.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is a root program?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The policy bundle a browser or OS maintains: which roots are trusted, and under what requirements (Chrome, Mozilla, Microsoft, Apple each run one).</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Do I need a paid certificate for e-commerce?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No: DV certificates provide identical encryption. Compliance and customer perception, not crypto, are the arguments for paid tiers.</p></details></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="related">Related</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2"><li><a href="/ssl-tls/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SSL/TLS hub →</a></li><li><a href="/caa-record/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">CAA records →</a></li><li><a href="/what-is-ssl-tls/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">What is SSL/TLS →</a></li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
